{
  "_comment_review_pii_example": "Review + PII documented example (not live — this file has no `review` section, so the review worker is off). To enable the PII pre-filter + auto-enqueue: add a `review` block with `auto_enqueue: true` (opt-in). The PII pre-filter is the deterministic `RegexPiiDetector` (the canonical `fluent_llm::pii_patterns` table behind `env/pii-patterns.json`'s builtins). `pii_threshold` (default 0.5) is the review candidate-flag floor. Fail-open: the pre-filter only ADDS spans to a review job, never drops a manually enqueued one. `POST /v1/sessions/{id}/review-parse` is unchanged. Example: {\"review_model\": \"onnx/llm\", \"queue_capacity\": 32, \"credit_limit\": 16, \"auto_enqueue\": true}. Review corrections are served by the onnx generative role (`onnx/llm`) with the `ParseReview` grammar.",
  "_comment_overlay_example": "Overlay documented example (not live — this file has no `overlay` section, so the overlay worker is off). To enable the lazy at-most-once node overlays: add an `overlay` block with `arc_ready: true` (opt-in). The `ArcReadyAnnotation` overlay (spacy-rs structural index), LLM enrichment, and embedding overlays are derived lazily, at-most-once, and in parallel from LOD0; the LLM enrichment uses the onnx generative role (`onnx/llm`) via `set_overlay_llm`. Fail-open: no overlay pipeline yields no overlays; a derivation failure never takes the request path down. Requires a ledger. Example: {\"arc_ready\": {}}.",
  "_comment_onnx_roles": "In-process ONNX fleet — the ONE model wiring point (ROADMAP_20260829_GENERAL_ONNX). The `onnx` section keys one role per model; this file ships the single generative role, `llm` (a `CausalLm` session — the general-purpose local LLM served behind the `fluent_llm::client::ChatBackend` seam as the default annotation / review / enrichment backend). Every role is optional; the pipeline is fully functional (pure-deterministic) with none loaded. The model runs in-process via ONNX Runtime (ort), never a spawned llama-server, and is supervised by the onnx residency loop (idle release + working-set LRU, sibling of the llama sidecar — the llama fleet stays on GPU). `execution_provider` selects the runtime: `cpu` (`intra_threads: 1` — deterministic decode) or `gpu`/`migraphx` (the AMD ROCm MIGraphX EP — successor to the ROCm EP removed upstream in ORT 1.23; the loader probes the linked onnxruntime and fails open to CPU with a loud warning if it lacks MIGraphX, so this file's `gpu` is honored only when a MIGraphX-enabled onnxruntime is linked via `ORT_LIB_PATH`). `resident: true` loads at boot (Always); `pinned` forbids release; `sleep_idle_seconds` controls idle unload; `params` are sampling defaults (per-request wins). A mismatched config.json / missing `logits` output is a loud boot error; absent onnx config is fully fail-open (the HTTP llama/deterministic ladder remains).",
  "_comment_onnx_instances": "ONNX context-declaration vocabulary (ROADMAP_20260830_LLMS M3) — LIVE for `onnx/llm` in this file. An onnx role may declare an `instances` block exactly like a llama `ModelEntry.instances` (same profile shape: name/group/count/num_ctx/parallel/pinned/warm/sleep_idle_seconds/default/resume/params/max_ctx), giving the onnx fleet the same 'one weights load, N named context windows, per-context KV' contract the llama fork already serves. Here the single `default` profile materializes one pinned 8192-token context window that stays resident (never evicted), bound to bare `onnx/llm` dispatch via `default: true`. `max_ctx` (per-profile or on the flattened model block / top-level `default_params`) caps how large a context may grow; a cap below `num_ctx` clamps the window at materialization. Absent `instances` keeps today's single-shot decode. Example: {\"instances\": {\"swarm\": {\"num_ctx\": 16384, \"count\": 3, \"group\": \"swarm\", \"max_ctx\": 8192}, \"ledger\": {\"num_ctx\": 131072, \"pinned\": true, \"default\": true}}}.",
  "_comment_max_ctx": "Context-size cap vocabulary (ROADMAP_20260830_LLMS M7) — `max_ctx` ships ABSENT everywhere in this file (behavior identical to today). It may appear on: (1) `default_params.max_ctx` — the global cap for every managed model that does not declare its own; (2) an instance profile's `max_ctx` (`models.<id>.instances.<name>.max_ctx`, or the onnx `instances` block) — the per-context cap; a cap below that context's `num_ctx` clamps the window at materialization. Resize-to-demand: a request that declares a bigger context window than the targeted context is allocated grows the context to fit (up to `max_ctx`); a request beyond `max_ctx` fails loudly with the same error a too-large llama request gets. Absent `max_ctx` = no cap (today's behavior).",
  "_comment_onnx": {
    "llm": {
      "resident": false,
      "pinned": false,
      "quantization": "q4",
